/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A stick diagram is a map, not a mask.
   - A poly line crossing diffusion always forms a transistor; poly cannot cross diffusion as plain wiring.
   - Shared source-drain regions show up in the sketch.
   - Write the gate as two networks.
   - One continuous diffusion strip per side is best: it removes extra contacts and breaks.
   - An Euler path exists only between the two vertices of odd degree.
   - For y = ¬(a·b + c) the two sides share the order c, a, b.
   - The stick diagram fixes topology; the chapter on layout design rules fixes dimensions.
   - For y = ¬(a·b + c) the finished static CMOS gate has 3 nMOS plus 3 pMOS transistors, so the mask layout must show 6 wanted poly-diffusion crossings.
*/
