/* book Worked example 2: Q: Draw the stick diagram of a two-input CMOS NAND gate y = ¬(a·b) and estimate the width of its layout. Use these λ rules: contact 2λ × 2λ with 1λ of diffusion beyond it, contact to gate 2λ, poly width 2λ, and 3λ between two gates that share an uncontacted node. Take λ = 0.5 μm.
   1. nMOS: a and b in series from y to VSS, through an internal node n1. pMOS: a and b in parallel from y to VDD.
   2. n-graph: a: y–n1, b: n1–VSS. Odd vertices y and VSS, so the path runs y–a–n1–b–VSS. Order a, b.
   3. p-graph: a: y–VDD, b: VDD–y. Every degree is 2, so the trail is closed: y–a–VDD–b–y. Same order a, b.
   4. Stick diagram: two poly columns a, b. p-strip: y | a | VDD | b | y, with contacts to y, VDD (rail) and y, and a metal line joining the two y ends. n-strip: y | a | n1 | b | VSS, with contacts to y and VSS; n1 needs no contact.
   5. p-strip width: 1 + 2 (contact) + 2 + 2 (gate a) + 2 + 2 (VDD contact) + 2 + 2 (gate b) + 2 + 2 (contact) + 1 = 20λ.
   6. n-strip width: 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 (gate a) + 3 (shared n1) + 2 (gate b) + 2 + 2 + 1 = 17λ.
   7. The wider strip sets the cell: 20λ = 20 × 0.5 μm = 10 μm.
   Answer: Column order a, b on both sides; the p-strip has three contacts and the n-strip two (n1 is shared diffusion with no contact). The cell is about 20λ = 10 μm wide, set by the p-strip. */
