/* book exam traps: A poly-diffusion crossing is always a transistor. Never route poly across diffusion as plain wiring. | Series and parallel swap between nMOS and pMOS. Write the dual; do not copy the nMOS network to the pMOS side. | An Euler trail runs between the two odd-degree vertices. When every degree is even the trail is closed, so both strip ends meet the same rail or node. | The same input order must be used on both sides; reversing one side breaks the shared poly columns. | Stick diagrams carry no dimensions, and a contact is not a transistor. Missing contacts leave a source floating; extra contacts short two nodes. */
