/* book exam traps: Adding a tap anywhere is not enough: what matters is the resistance from the injection point to the nearest tap. A distant tap leaves a large Rsub or Rwell. | Latch-up is not the same as ESD damage. ESD is a fast overvoltage event; latch-up is a sustained low-resistance path that remains after the trigger. | The loop condition is the product βnpn × βpnp > 1, not the sum. One weak device does not make the loop safe: βnpn = 5 with βpnp = 0.5 still gives 2.5 > 1. | A tap does not replace a guard ring. The ring lowers local resistance and diverts carriers, while the tap sets the reference potential. | In the current-injection test, the chip has latched only if the supply current stays high after the injected current is removed; a temporary rise is not latch-up. */
